1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,146
Terry Tibbs turns your speech into text on your Mac. Hold

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:06,293
a shortcut, speak, then release. It copies your words and tries

3
00:00:06,293 --> 00:00:09,439
to paste them into the text box where you started. No

4
00:00:09,439 --> 00:00:12,585
send button is pressed.

5
00:00:13,585 --> 00:00:18,059
First, open Settings. Record a shortcut, or choose Globe and Function.

6
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:22,533
Pick your microphone and use Test input to check it. Save

7
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:27,006
your settings. Allow Microphone and Accessibility when macOS asks.

8
00:00:28,006 --> 00:00:31,907
Click the text box you want to write in. Hold your

9
00:00:31,907 --> 00:00:35,808
shortcut and speak when Terry is listening. Release to finish. This

10
00:00:35,808 --> 00:00:39,709
demonstration feeds a prerecorded sentence through the real local speech engine.

11
00:00:40,709 --> 00:00:44,169
Here is the actual result. If Terry says Copied only, your

12
00:00:44,169 --> 00:00:47,629
words are still safe on the clipboard. Click the text box

13
00:00:47,629 --> 00:00:51,089
and press Command V. Check Accessibility and keep the original app

14
00:00:51,089 --> 00:00:54,548
focused for automatic paste.

15
00:00:55,548 --> 00:00:59,821
For longer thoughts, double-tap your shortcut. Locked means you can let

16
00:00:59,821 --> 00:01:04,093
go and keep talking. Press once more to finish. Press Escape

17
00:01:04,093 --> 00:01:08,366
to cancel instead. Each recording can be up to two minutes.

18
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:13,034
Close the window to keep Terry in the menu bar. Open

19
00:01:13,034 --> 00:01:16,703
it again from the microphone icon. Quit stops it completely. Installation

20
00:01:16,703 --> 00:01:20,372
instructions are below this video. After setup and the model download,

21
00:01:20,372 --> 00:01:24,041
dictation works offline.
